1
00:00:04,340 --> 00:00:07,620
Hello everyone and welcome to another lesson here at EnglishPod.

2
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:08,880
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:14,580
And I'm Catherine and today we have an elementary level lesson all about life with a roommate.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,580
That's right.

5
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:21,980
So sometimes during college you have to live with a roommate because living alone is too expensive.

6
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:29,980
Also, after college I know that a lot of people who are just starting their jobs often live with a roommate to save some money.

7
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:30,590
That's right.

8
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:34,650
Or maybe your girlfriend could be considered kind of like your romantic roommate.

9
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:39,170
I hope not in the sense of today's lesson.

10
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:40,910
We're talking about problems with a roommate.

11
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:44,310
So let's listen to this dialogue and we'll be back in a moment.

12
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:51,250
Charlie, do you have a second?

13
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,040
Yeah, what's up?

14
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:57,440
Well, I went and paid the bills today and you still haven't given me your half.

15
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,440
Yeah, I wanted to talk to you about that.

16
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:05,230
I agreed we would go halves on all the bills, but frankly I think it's unfair.

17
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:06,190
Unfair?

18
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:07,050
Why?

19
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:11,190
Well, you have long hair and use the hair dryer every morning.

20
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:11,830
I don't.

21
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:15,130
You leave your computer on all night downloading torrents.

22
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:15,790
I don't.

23
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:17,390
You see what I'm getting at here?

24
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:19,890
You leave the air conditioner on day and night.

25
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:24,550
You also take 30 minute showers, which means you're using way more gas and water than me.

26
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,760
Well, while we're on the subject, stop bringing your friends over for drinks every weekend.

27
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:32,800
You always leave a mess and keep me up all night.

28
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,920
Maybe you should just move out and find another place.

29
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:37,820
Maybe you should move out.

30
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:44,100
All right, we're back.

31
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:49,590
So we have a couple of different words that we're going to take a look at now in Language Takeaway.

32
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:52,320
Language Takeaway.

33
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:58,860
So the first word is a dreaded word that means that most people don't like it.

34
00:01:59,000 --> 00:01:59,740
They fear it.

35
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:00,600
This is bills.

36
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:01,400
That's right.

37
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:03,480
You have to pay your bills.

38
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:08,340
So a bill is the paper that tells you how much you have to pay.

39
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:09,160
That's right.

40
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:10,780
So you can have a gas bill.

41
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:12,180
An electricity bill.

42
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:13,400
A cable bill.

43
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:16,460
Or even at the restaurant, you ask for the bill.

44
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:20,080
All right, so this is the amount of money you need to pay.

45
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:21,560
That's right.

46
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:29,920
And usually at the end of the month, you receive many, many bills in the mail and you have to pay the companies like the gas company and the electricity company.

47
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:30,080
That's right.

48
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:31,100
So those are bills.

49
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,520
A singular would be a bill.

50
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:36,420
And so this is how the argument starts.

51
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:43,760
The two roommates decide that they have to pay the bills, but they don't want to split the bills in half, go halves.

52
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,140
They have to pay their own.

53
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:49,290
And so one person says, listen, this isn't fair.

54
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:54,170
You leave your computer on all night downloading torrents.

55
00:02:54,390 --> 00:03:01,070
Okay, so we understand what it is to download something or you download this podcast, but a torrent, what is that?

56
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:02,010
That's kind of technical.

57
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:02,870
That is.

58
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:07,490
So a torrent is a kind of file that you can download from the internet.

59
00:03:08,190 --> 00:03:23,440
So basically when you want to download a movie or some music from the internet, you go to a website that will connect you to someone who owns this movie or book or music and they'll have a special file that's called a torrent file and that's what you download.

60
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:24,300
That's right.

61
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:31,240
So it's a new way of downloading files off the internet from many people, kind of like a peer sharing.

62
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:32,000
That's right.

63
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,080
So when one person disappears or goes offline, it shares you with another person.

64
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:36,640
That's right.

65
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:38,340
So those are torrents.

66
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:43,120
And now, well, they kept arguing about you have to pay this, I have to pay that.

67
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:46,140
So you said, you know what, you should just move out.

68
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:53,180
Ooh, so to move out, this is a phrase that has two words to move out.

69
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:56,620
It means to leave this house forever, this apartment.

70
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:59,380
So that means to find a new place to live.

71
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,600
Right.

72
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:02,320
So I can say, oh, I'm moving out this weekend.

73
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,220
That means I'm changing apartments.

74
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:05,560
Right.

75
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:07,300
Or my boyfriend just moved out.

76
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:07,940
Okay.

77
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:08,940
That's really sad.

78
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:10,880
He left your house.

79
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:11,680
Okay.

80
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,240
So that is to move out.

81
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:19,300
And now we have a word that is more colloquial of how to say a house or an apartment.

82
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:21,160
This is place.

83
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:22,300
Okay.

84
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:22,820
So you just say a place.

85
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:24,720
So place normally is a location.

86
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:26,480
Okay.

87
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,920
Where is this place?

88
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,320
But we could say, hey, we're going to have dinner at my place.

89
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:32,420
Would you like to come?

90
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:35,180
So that means we're going to have dinner at my house.

91
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:35,700
Right.

92
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:37,020
My place, his place.

93
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:37,680
His place.

94
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,960
Or you have a really nice place.

95
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:40,500
Thanks.

96
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:41,240
Okay.

97
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,280
So that means you have a really nice house.

98
00:04:43,280 --> 00:04:44,280
Exactly.

99
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:45,280
Okay.

100
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:50,010
So four key words today when we listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back in a little bit.

101
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:55,930
Charlie, do you have a second?

102
00:04:56,770 --> 00:04:57,690
Yeah, what's up?

103
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:02,090
Well, I went and paid the bills today and you still haven't given me your half.

104
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:05,070
Yeah, I wanted to talk to you about that.

105
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:09,830
I agreed we would go halves on all the bills, but frankly, I think it's unfair.

106
00:05:10,370 --> 00:05:10,810
Unfair?

107
00:05:11,390 --> 00:05:11,690
Why?

108
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:15,830
Well, you have long hair and use the hairdryer every morning.

109
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:16,470
I don't.

110
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,770
You leave your computer on all night downloading torrents.

111
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:20,410
I don't.

112
00:05:20,870 --> 00:05:22,010
You see what I'm getting at here?

113
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:24,770
You leave the air conditioner on day and night.

114
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:29,430
You also take 30 minute showers, which means you're using way more gas and water than me.

115
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:34,660
Well, while we're on the subject, stop bringing your friends over for drinks every weekend.

116
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,720
You always leave a mess and keep me up all night.

117
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,840
Maybe you should just move out and find another place.

118
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:42,720
Maybe you should move out.

119
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:51,600
All right, we are back and we've got some great phrases today coming up in Fluency Builders.

120
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:52,620
Let's take a look at some of those.

121
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:54,720
Fluency Builder.

122
00:05:55,440 --> 00:06:04,760
All right, so the first phrase that we have, very common, very, very common, but also very difficult for English learners to answer this question.

123
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:06,440
All right, what's up?

124
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:06,590
What's up?

125
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,410
So if somebody says, what's up, what does that mean?

126
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,410
That means what's going on?

127
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:12,170
What's new?

128
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:13,920
How are you?

129
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:14,920
How is it going?

130
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:15,920
Okay.

131
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:16,920
Hi.

132
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:17,920
So how should you answer this?

133
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:23,540
Okay, so think of what's up as being a question about what's new, what's happening.

134
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:25,680
You can say, what's up?

135
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:26,920
Oh, not much.

136
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,440
Not much.

137
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:30,440
Okay, what's up means what's new?

138
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:31,520
Oh, not much is new.

139
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:31,960
Not much.

140
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:32,860
Everything is the same.

141
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:34,840
So what's up?

142
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:36,620
You could say, hey, hi, how are you?

143
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:38,120
It's another way to say hi.

144
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:38,820
Okay.

145
00:06:39,220 --> 00:06:40,820
But you can't say, this guy.

146
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:48,000
Right, or sometimes English learners will say, fine, thank you, because they think that what's up means how are you?

147
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:49,300
No, that's different.

148
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:54,100
So you cannot say, fine, thank you, fine, well.

149
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:59,320
These are words that answer how are you questions, but this isn't a how are you question.

150
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,960
This is a what's new question.

151
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:03,740
What's new or hi.

152
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,200
Okay, so somebody says, what's up?

153
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:09,380
You can say, not much, or you can say, hey, what's up?

154
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:10,480
You can say, what's up back?

155
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,200
What's up?

156
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:13,180
What's up with you?

157
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:14,200
Yeah, what's up with you?

158
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,400
Okay, so that's our first phrase.

159
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:24,420
Now, when the guy was talking about the hair dryer and the computer, he finished off that paragraph by saying, you see what I'm getting at here?

160
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:25,960
Okay, you're doing this.

161
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:29,160
You use the hair dryer, you leave the computer on.

162
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:31,120
Do you see what I'm getting at here?

163
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,660
This phrase, do you see what I'm getting at?

164
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,360
It means, do you understand what I'm talking about?

165
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:37,340
Do you see my point?

166
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:43,680
That means I haven't said exactly what I mean, but you should understand what I'm saying.

167
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,540
So he's saying, you're wasting electricity.

168
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:50,320
You use all of these services more than I do.

169
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:54,600
And we could say, maybe Marco didn't get a good grade in his English test last week.

170
00:07:54,600 --> 00:08:02,610
I say, listen, maybe it has to do with your study habits or how much you love video games or how little time you spend at school.

171
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:04,030
Do you see what I'm getting at?

172
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:07,190
Okay, so you're not saying directly that I don't study.

173
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:10,850
You're just telling me the reasons, maybe why as to why I'm not studying.

174
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:12,160
Like a hint.

175
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:16,590
Okay, so that's what you say when you don't tell somebody directly what the problem is.

176
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:18,460
You say, you see what I'm getting at here?

177
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:20,240
Do you see what I'm getting at?

178
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:22,160
Getting at means hinting.

179
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:33,820
Okay, and our last phrase, when they kept talking about the bills, one of the person said, you are using way more gas and water than me.

180
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:35,800
So what about this way more?

181
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:37,180
Way more.

182
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:40,900
Way means much or a lot.

183
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:44,620
So we could say, you are using a lot more water than I am.

184
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:47,020
Or you're using way more water.

185
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:48,920
This is very spoken language.

186
00:08:48,920 --> 00:08:49,920
This is not written language.

187
00:08:50,060 --> 00:08:51,320
So don't write this down on your tests.

188
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:54,960
But way more means a lot more, much more.

189
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,940
Okay, you can say, Bob is way more attractive than Bill.

190
00:08:58,520 --> 00:08:58,750
That's right.

191
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:01,590
Or this is way more interesting than that show.

192
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:08,390
Okay, so you can say way more instead of much more or a lot more, but it's usually spoken English.

193
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:10,120
Exactly.

194
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:15,920
All right, let's listen to our dialogue for the last time and we'll be back in a bit to talk a little bit more.

195
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:21,200
Charlie, do you have a second?

196
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:22,440
Yeah, what's up?

197
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,820
Well, I went and paid the bills today and you still haven't given me your half.

198
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,820
Yeah, I wanted to talk to you about that.

199
00:09:30,350 --> 00:09:34,590
I agreed we would go halves on all the bills, but frankly, I think it's unfair.

200
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:35,590
Unfair?

201
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:36,450
Why?

202
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,590
Well, you have long hair and use the hairdryer every morning.

203
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:41,230
I don't.

204
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:44,530
You leave your computer on all night downloading torrents.

205
00:09:44,690 --> 00:09:45,190
I don't.

206
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:46,790
You see what I'm getting at here?

207
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:49,270
You leave the air conditioner on day and night.

208
00:09:49,590 --> 00:09:53,930
You also take 30 minute showers, which means you're using way more gas and water than me.

209
00:09:54,510 --> 00:09:59,170
Well, while we're on the subject, stop bringing your friends over for drinks every weekend.

210
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:02,210
You always leave a mess and keep me up all night.

211
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:05,310
Maybe you should just move out and find another place.

212
00:10:05,890 --> 00:10:07,190
Maybe you should move out.

213
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:16,780
So Marco, have you ever had any of these problems with a roommate?

214
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:22,280
I've actually stayed away from roommates because of issues like this.

215
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:26,660
You mean you have not had many roommates because you're afraid of this situation?

216
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:37,950
Yeah, I've always actually tried to live on my own by myself, like in a small apartment or studio, or if I have to have a roommate, maybe just one, not a house full of roommates.

217
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:41,270
Because I think the more people there are, the little bit more complicated it gets.

218
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:43,510
And if it's just two, it's a little bit simpler.

219
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:48,830
This is true, although sometimes I think it depends on the people that you're sharing a house with.

220
00:10:49,440 --> 00:11:02,710
I've had one awkward experience where people did not want to pay ever, and they were always late with their payments, and one situation where people were very good about it, so always remembered and were very helpful.

221
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:03,870
So it really depends.

222
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:08,500
Yeah, I think it's hard to live with someone always, of course.

223
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:14,460
Even if you start living with your girlfriend or boyfriend or you get married, it's kind of different to live with someone.

224
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:20,880
And especially money-wise, I think it always complicates things with people.

225
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:21,180
Definitely.

226
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:22,320
So I think you're right, though.

227
00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:25,060
If there's two people, it's very easy to share the bills.

228
00:11:25,720 --> 00:11:32,360
But if there's five people or six people, it's very hard to find out who spent more money or what the rules are.

229
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:34,670
Yeah, so it can get a little bit complicated.

230
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:41,650
But I'm sure a lot of our listeners have probably had roommates before, maybe in college, maybe even now.

231
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:48,360
So let's hear those experiences and maybe those good and bad stories about living with other people.

232
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:49,550
So please get in touch with us.

233
